From: AROS eza port maintainers
Date: 2026-10-21
Subject: [PATCH] AROS: POSIX dot operands, volume-root names and Git matching
Repository: eza
Base-Commit: 98442ab17c2c3738701b62a7e060b1431ae2d6ea
Preceding-Patches: 0001-0009
Upstream-Status: AROS-specific, not for upstream
Scope: target_os = "aros" only; helpers tested on the host.

Operands: 0009 replaced only ".". An operand is now kept when it exists as
written (AmigaDOS allows files named "." and ".."); otherwise a POSIX form
is tried in AmigaDOS terms ("./x" -> "x", "../x" -> "/x", "a/../b" ->
"a//b", ".." -> "/") and used when that exists. Anything else is kept, so
error messages name what was typed. "." is still the current drawer.

Names: an operand at a volume root ("RAM:clean.txt") was shown as
"clean.txt", because Rust's Path sees one component and no parent there.
The volume part is now printed in the parent-path style.

Git: GitRepo::has_path used Path::starts_with, which compares components,
and Rust sees "RAM:x" as one component; files listed in a volume root were
never matched to the repository there, so --git showed nothing and
--git-ignore kept ignored files. The AROS check compares bytes at a ':' or
'/' boundary (aros_policy::path_starts_with).

Verification: host tests posix_path_tests and prefix_tests in src/aros_policy.rs; native
results in the port's validation documents.
Removal: with the AROS path patches.

diff --git a/src/aros.rs b/src/aros.rs
index c3dfa66..41cf641 100644
--- a/src/aros.rs
+++ b/src/aros.rs
@@ -131,7 +131,23 @@ pub fn exit(code: i32) -> ! {
     std::process::exit(code)
 }
 
-/// The current directory by name, for operands that say ".".
-pub fn current_dir_name() -> std::ffi::OsString {
-    std::env::current_dir().map_or_else(|_| ".".into(), std::path::PathBuf::into_os_string)
+/// An operand as eza should open it. "." is the current drawer by name
+/// (AmigaDOS has no "." entry and the C library does not translate it). A
+/// path that exists as written is kept, because "." and ".." are legal
+/// AmigaDOS names; otherwise a POSIX form ("./x", "../x", "a/../b") is
+/// translated when the translation exists. Anything else is kept, so that
+/// error messages name what the user typed.
+pub fn operand(path: &std::ffi::OsStr) -> std::ffi::OsString {
+    use std::os::unix::ffi::{OsStrExt, OsStringExt};
+    if path == "." {
+        return std::env::current_dir().map_or_else(|_| path.into(), std::path::PathBuf::into_os_string);
+    }
+    if canonicalize(std::path::Path::new(path)).is_some() {
+        return path.into();
+    }
+    match crate::aros_policy::from_posix(path.as_bytes()) {
+        Some(t) if t.is_empty() => std::env::current_dir().map_or_else(|_| path.into(), std::path::PathBuf::into_os_string),
+        Some(t) if canonicalize(std::path::Path::new(std::ffi::OsStr::from_bytes(&t))).is_some() => std::ffi::OsString::from_vec(t),
+        _ => path.into(),
+    }
 }
